<%# Figures.

  A `metric` IS READ AT RENDER TIME, a `value` is a literal the operator typed. That is what keeps "nine
  channels" correct on the day a tenth is connected -- the number is not stored anywhere an edit could make
  it stale. A metric nobody implemented renders nothing rather than a blank figure with a label.

  SET AS A SPECIFICATION ROW, not as three big numbers. On the home page this section follows the hero, and
  three large figures there would compete with the headline for the same glance -- the reading these deserve
  is "what this product supports", which is a spec line, so they get a rule above them, a quiet size, and
  equal weight with their labels.

  THAT RULE IS THE ONLY ONE BETWEEN THIS ROW AND THE HERO, and the gap above it is the page's own section
  step. The row sat at `mt-10`, off the `mt-16` every other section uses, and its rule landed 48px below a
  `border-b` the hero drew for itself -- two hairlines that close to each other read as a mistake rather than
  as a boundary. The hero no longer closes itself (sections/_hero.html.erb); this rule is the boundary.
%>
<% items = Array(section['items']).filter_map do |item|
     figure = item['metric'].present? ? marketing_metric(item['metric']) : item['value']
     next if figure.blank?

     { figure: figure, label: item['label'] }
   end %>

<% if items.any? %>
  <dl class="mt-16 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-6 border-t border-lyn-line pt-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <% items.each do |item| %>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
        <dt class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-lyn-ink"><%= item[:figure] %></dt>
        <dd class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-lyn-ink-soft"><%= item[:label] %></dd>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </dl>
<% end %>
